---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro'
import PageHero from '../../components/PageHero.astro'
import ActionBox from '../../components/ActionBox.astro'
import ServiceEntitlement from '../../components/ServiceEntitlement.astro'
---

<Base
  title="The status service"
  description="The live health of the OIML SMART estate: twelve probed services with content assertions, declared objectives against measured actuals, a human-written incident record, and RSS/Atom subscription."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="Services"
    title="The status service"
    lede="One page answers 'is it up?': every public service in the estate probed once a minute, the declared availability and latency objectives next to the measured numbers, and a human-written incident record. No account needed, ever."
  />

  <section class="px-8 pb-20 max-w-5xl">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">What it is</h2>
      <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-4">
        The estate's health surface. Twelve probe rows cover the identity service (discovery and sign-in separately), the platform hub and the demo/NMI/TL instances, the AI assistant, and the public surfaces (this site, the vocabulary, the studio viewer, the CNML docs, the concepts registry). A probe is not a bare 200: each row carries a content assertion, so a page that answers with the wrong document fails honestly.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft mb-4">
        Each row publishes its declared objective next to the measured actual (30/90-day uptime, 30-day p95 latency), and a number is never rounded up to meet a target. The incident record is written by a human after the fact, published from the incident rows in the service's repository: the window, the impact, the root cause, the resolution, the follow-ups. When nothing has broken, it says so.
      </p>
      <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        Subscribing is a feed act today: <a href="https://status.oimlsmart.org/feed.xml" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2">RSS 2.0</a> or <a href="https://status.oimlsmart.org/feed.atom" class="text-accent underline underline-offset-2">Atom</a>. Email subscription rides the estate mailer posture and lands with the status tail wave; until then the page says feeds, because feeds are what exist.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">Who it's for</h2>
      <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        Anyone whose work depends on the estate: the officer about to present the demo, the laboratory mid-evaluation, the applicant checking a certificate link, and the operators themselves, who read the same page you do.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">Where it lives</h2>
      <ActionBox
        href="https://status.oimlsmart.org/"
        label="status.oimlsmart.org"
        description="The live estate health. Public; the incident timeline is subscribable as RSS or Atom."
      />
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">How to get access</h2>
      <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        Open the page, or point a feed reader at the RSS/Atom URLs above. There is no account tier; the status service is the one page that must work when everything else is down, so it asks for nothing.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">Status</h2>
      <p class="text-[1.0625rem] leading-relaxed text-ink-soft">
        The status service does not probe itself: its page is the probe output, and adding a row for itself would assert what it cannot independently check. Its software (<span class="font-mono text-sm">oimlsmart/status</span>) is a member repository, source available to members.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-[clamp(1.5rem,3vw,2rem)] font-medium tracking-[-0.01em] mb-4">Who can use it, who can run it</h2>
      <ServiceEntitlement service="status-service" />
    </div>

    <div class="border-t border-rule pt-8 max-w-3xl">
      <p class="text-sm text-ink-muted">
        Verified 2026-08-29: the page answered live with all twelve probe rows operational, the declared-versus-actual objectives table, the empty incident record, and both feeds (RSS and Atom) returning 200.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
